### What is log retention?

Time-and-size retention is the default cleanup policy, configured with `retention.ms` (how long to keep records) and `retention.bytes` (maximum partition size). Kafka stores a partition as a series of segment files. When the oldest segment's records exceed the retention time, or the partition exceeds the retention size, Kafka deletes that whole segment. Retention does not look at keys at all; it simply removes old data in bulk.

```java
package com.bank.retention;

public class RetentionPolicyExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Retention Policy (cleanup.policy=delete):");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("retention.ms = 604800000 (7 days)");
        System.out.println("retention.bytes = -1 (unlimited size)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Day 1: [transfer-1] [transfer-2] [transfer-3]");
        System.out.println("Day 7: [transfer-1..1000] (7 days of transfers)");
        System.out.println("Day 8: [transfer-100..1000] (day 1 segment deleted)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("All keys affected equally. No key awareness.");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Retention: keeps records for 7 days, then deletes old segments.
No key awareness. All keys age out equally.
```

### What is log compaction?

Log compaction keeps, for each distinct key, at least the latest record and removes older records with the same key. The result is that the partition always holds the latest value for every key that has ever been written, so it acts like a durable key-value snapshot.

```java
package com.bank.compaction;

public class CompactionExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Compaction (cleanup.policy=compact):");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Before compaction:");
        System.out.println("  offset 0: key=SAV2001 balance=10000");
        System.out.println("  offset 1: key=SAV3002 balance=25000");
        System.out.println("  offset 2: key=SAV2001 balance=15000");
        System.out.println("  offset 3: key=SAV2001 balance=20000");
        System.out.println("  offset 4: key=SAV3002 balance=30000");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("After compaction:");
        System.out.println("  offset 3: key=SAV2001 balance=20000 (latest)");
        System.out.println("  offset 4: key=SAV3002 balance=30000 (latest)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Offsets 0, 1, 2 removed (older versions of same keys)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Before: 5 records (3 for SAV2001, 2 for SAV3002)
After: 2 records (latest per key)
Older versions removed by log cleaner.
```

### How is retention different from compaction? (comparison table)

| Feature | Retention (delete) | Compaction (compact) |
|---|---|---|
| Trigger | Age or size threshold | Duplicate keys |
| What is removed | Entire old segments | Older records per key |
| Key awareness | No | Yes |
| Preserves history | Rolling window (recent) | No (only latest per key) |
| Preserves latest per key | Not guaranteed | Guaranteed |
| Storage growth | Bounded by time/size | Bounded by unique key count |
| Use case | Event streams (transfers, logs) | State snapshots (balances, configs) |

```java
package com.bank.comparison;

public class RetentionVsCompaction {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Retention: rolling window of full event history");
        System.out.println("  transfers topic: 7 days of all transfers");
        System.out.println("  After 7 days, old transfers deleted");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Compaction: latest value per key forever");
        System.out.println("  account-balances topic: current balance per account");
        System.out.println("  Old balance updates removed, latest kept");
    }
}
```

```text
Retention: keeps recent history, removes by age
Compaction: keeps latest per key, removes older duplicates
```

### What is a tombstone in compaction?

A record with a key and a null value is called a tombstone. It signals that the key should be deleted. Compaction keeps the tombstone for `delete.retention.ms` so downstream consumers learn the key was removed, then eventually purges it.

```java
package com.bank.compaction;

import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class AccountBalancePublisher {

    private final KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate;

    public AccountBalancePublisher(
            KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate) {
        this.kafkaTemplate = kafkaTemplate;
    }

    public void updateBalance(String accountId, String balanceJson) {
        kafkaTemplate.send("account-balances", accountId, balanceJson);
        System.out.println("Balance updated: " + accountId);
    }

    public void closeAccount(String accountId) {
        // Tombstone: null value signals deletion
        kafkaTemplate.send("account-balances", accountId, null);
        System.out.println("Tombstone sent for closed account: " + accountId);
        // Downstream caches will drop this key after reading the tombstone
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Balance updated: SAV2001
Tombstone sent for closed account: SAV2001

Compacted topic after tombstone:
  SAV2001: null (tombstone, kept for delete.retention.ms)
  SAV3002: {"balance":30000} (latest balance)

After delete.retention.ms expires:
  SAV2001: removed entirely
  SAV3002: {"balance":30000}
```

### When do you use retention vs compaction?

Event topics are historical facts ("a transfer happened"), so they use retention with a window sized for replay and compliance. State topics are current values ("this account's balance"), so they use compaction. You can combine both with `compact,delete`.

```java
package com.bank.config;

public class CleanupPolicyDecision {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Cleanup Policy Decision:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Event topics (cleanup.policy=delete):");
        System.out.println("  transfer-events: retention 7 days");
        System.out.println("  audit-events: retention 90 days (compliance)");
        System.out.println("  app-logs: retention 3 days");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("State topics (cleanup.policy=compact):");
        System.out.println("  account-balances: latest balance per account");
        System.out.println("  customer-profiles: latest profile per customer");
        System.out.println("  __consumer_offsets: latest offset per group");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Both (cleanup.policy=compact,delete):");
        System.out.println("  session-state: compact recent + delete old");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Events: retention (delete old by time)
State: compaction (keep latest per key)
Both: compact,delete (compact recent, delete very old)
```

### What are common mistakes with cleanup policies?

The most dangerous mistake is compacting an event topic, which silently drops intermediate transactions. Another mistake is using plain retention for a state topic, which eventually deletes the only copy of a current balance.

```java
package com.bank.mistakes;

// WRONG: Compacting an event feed.
public class WrongCompactionOnEvents {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: cleanup.policy=compact on transfer-events");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Before compaction:");
        System.out.println("  SAV2001: DEBIT  50000 (offset 0)");
        System.out.println("  SAV2001: CREDIT 20000 (offset 1)");
        System.out.println("  SAV2001: DEBIT  30000 (offset 2)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("After compaction:");
        System.out.println("  SAV2001: DEBIT 30000 (only offset 2 kept)");
        System.out.println("  Offsets 0 and 1 SILENTLY DROPPED!");
        System.out.println("  Audit trail broken, transfers lost from history");
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.mistakes;

// CORRECT: Retention for events, compaction for state.
public class CorrectCleanupPolicy {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("CORRECT:");
        System.out.println("  transfer-events: cleanup.policy=delete, retention=7d");
        System.out.println("  -> All transfers kept for 7 days (full history)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  account-balances: cleanup.policy=compact");
        System.out.println("  -> Latest balance per account kept forever");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: compacting events = silently drops intermediate transactions
CORRECT: retention for events, compaction for state
```

### How does compaction work operationally?

The log cleaner background thread scans the partition and builds a map of keys to their latest offset. It then rewrites the older segments, keeping only the latest record per key. There is always a head segment that has not yet been compacted.

```java
package com.bank.operations;

public class CompactionOperations {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Compaction Operations:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("min.cleanable.dirty.ratio = 0.5");
        System.out.println("  Cleaner runs when 50% of log is dirty (uncompacted)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("delete.retention.ms = 86400000 (24 hours)");
        System.out.println("  Tombstones kept for 24 hours before removal");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Active segment: never compacted (head of log)");
        System.out.println("  Recent writes may have duplicate keys temporarily");
        System.out.println("  Consumer reading from start sees latest after compaction");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Cleaner runs when 50% dirty. Tombstones kept 24h.
Active segment not compacted (temporary duplicates possible).
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The classic pattern is event sourcing plus a compacted state topic: raw events live in a retention topic for audit and replay, while a derived compacted topic holds the current materialized state for fast lookups.

```java
package com.bank.senior;

public class EventSourcingPlusCompaction {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Event Sourcing + Compacted State:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("transfer-events (cleanup.policy=delete, 90 days):");
        System.out.println("  Full history of every transfer");
        System.out.println("  Used for: audit, replay, compliance");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("account-balances (cleanup.policy=compact):");
        System.out.println("  Derived from transfer-events via Kafka Streams");
        System.out.println("  Latest balance per account");
        System.out.println("  Used for: fast current-value lookups");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Both topics serve different needs from same data.");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
transfer-events (retention): full history for audit
account-balances (compaction): latest value for lookups
```
